{#
  T-7.07 — the story view. A modal over the feed, not a page and not a route.

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.stories.story-modal' with { story: item, id: 'story-1' } only %}

  Parameters:
    story   required — one entry from the story collection
    id      required — matches the `data-sheet-open` on the card that opens it

  IT IS THE T-2.10 PRIMITIVE, NOT A SECOND OVERLAY. Everything this task is
  graded on — focus in, focus trapped, focus returned to the card, Esc, backdrop,
  scroll lock — is the primitive's, which in turn is the browser's `<dialog>`.
  This file arranges content inside it.

  «Dismissal returns focus to the originating feed card at its prior scroll
  position» is met without a line of script here. `showModal()` restores focus to
  the element that had it, which is the card's own button; and the scroll lock is
  `overflow: hidden` rather than `position: fixed`, so the document keeps its
  offset while the modal is open. **The position-fixed lock is the one that loses
  the scroll position, and this criterion is the reason T-2.10 does not use it.**

  THE HOTSPOT IS THE T-4.06 COMPONENT. The owner's B6 ruling makes that
  mandatory: "one hotspot-and-pill component serves T-4.06 and T-7.07; a second
  implementation is a defect." So the same `components.ui.hotspot-image` renders
  here, in its `overlay` layout, and `partials/hotspots.js` fills the pill from
  the product id at runtime.

  ONE POINT, PASSED AS A LIST OF ONE. `twilight.json` supports no nested
  collection, so a story item carries a single `hotspot_x`/`hotspot_y`/
  `product` triple rather than a list — the schema limit T-4.06 recorded. The
  primitive takes a list, so it is handed a list with one entry, and it needs no
  special case. **A story with no product renders the image and no marker**,
  because the list is then empty and the primitive draws nothing.

  NO HEADING, NO `Article` SCHEMA. The artboard draws neither, and the owner
  confirmed on 2026-08-06 that the story view is not an article. The dialog is
  still *named* — `title_hidden` keeps the accessible name and drops the pixels,
  because an unnamed dialog is announced as "dialog" and nothing else.

  FROM 1024px IT IS THE DESKTOP POP-UP. `Desktop Customer Stories - Story
  Pop-up.svg` draws the same story as a portrait card between two square
  chevrons, with the neighbouring stories smaller on either side. The chevrons
  are rendered here, `hidden` until `partials/story-modal.js` knows there is a
  story to step to; the neighbours are drawn by that script from the feed's own
  cards. Below 1024px neither is displayed — the phone artboards draw neither.
#}
{#
  The story's product, resolved ONCE for the hotspot and the favourite button.

  `product` is the customiser's product picker; its value arrives as a list even
  with one choice allowed, and an entry may be the product itself or only its
  id. `product_id` is a typed id saved before the picker existed, kept as the
  fallback so no merchant's existing story goes blank. The footer read only
  `product_id` after the picker landed, so «أضف للمفضلة» stopped rendering for
  every story set up since (audit, 2026-10-15).
#}
{% set story_product = story.product is defined and story.product ? story.product : null %}
{% if story_product is iterable and story_product.id is not defined %}
    {% set story_product = story_product|first %}
{% endif %}
{% if story_product %}
    {% set story_product_id = story_product.id is defined ? story_product.id : story_product %}
{% else %}
    {% set story_product_id = story.product_id|default('') %}
{% endif %}
{% set story_points = story_product_id
    ? [{ x: story.hotspot_x|default(50), y: story.hotspot_y|default(50), product_id: story_product_id }]
    : [] %}
{% set story_title = story.brand
    ? trans('theme.stories.story_of', { name: story.brand })
    : trans('theme.stories.story') %}

{% set story_body %}
    {% include 'components.ui.hotspot-image' with {
        image:    story.image,
        alt:      story.alt,
        layout:   'overlay',
        hotspots: story_points,
        id:       id ~ '-hotspot',
        defer:    true
    } only %}

    {% include 'components.stories.story-tags' with { story: story } only %}

    {#
      The desktop pop-up's neighbours and chevrons. The neighbours are pictures
      of other stories and nothing more — `aria-hidden`, never focusable — so
      the chevrons are the one route to the next story for a keyboard or a
      screen reader, and the pictures a shortcut for a pointer.

      Previous before next in the source, which is also their reading order.
      Each chevron points the way it moves: in Arabic previous stands at the
      right and next at the left, as drawn, and LTR mirrors both in the
      stylesheet rather than here.
    #}
    <div class="story-modal__peek story-modal__peek--prev" data-story-peek="prev" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="story-modal__peek story-modal__peek--next" data-story-peek="next" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <button type="button"
            class="story-modal__nav story-modal__nav--prev"
            data-story-step="-1"
            aria-label="{{ trans('theme.stories.previous') }}"
            hidden>
        {% include 'components.ui.icon' with { name: 'keyboard_arrow_right' } only %}
    </button>
    <button type="button"
            class="story-modal__nav story-modal__nav--next"
            data-story-step="1"
            aria-label="{{ trans('theme.stories.next') }}"
            hidden>
        {% include 'components.ui.icon' with { name: 'keyboard_arrow_left' } only %}
    </button>
{% endset %}

{% set story_footer %}
    {#
      «إغلاق» first and «أضف للمفضلة» second, which in Arabic puts close at the
      right — the artboard's order.

      The favourite button is `btn--wishlist` with a `data-id`, which is the
      selector `wishlist.js` already syncs. That is not a styling choice: it means
      the true state arrives from `salla.wishlist.event` and is corrected from
      storage on every page load, so this button cannot claim a product is
      favourited when the request failed. T-4.01 found that bug and this inherits
      the fix rather than repeating the mistake.

      It renders in the not-added state and is corrected on ready — the theme
      cannot know the wishlist server-side, and guessing would be worse than
      being briefly behind.

      `btn--neutral`, not the brand outline: both buttons are drawn white with a
      grey boundary and grey ink (`fill #FDFDFD`, ink `#646361`), which is the
      T-2.11 variant. `.btn--outline-primary` painted them in the merchant's
      colour. The added state's red still wins — `.btn--wishlist.is-added` is
      the more specific rule.
    #}
    <button type="button" class="btn btn--neutral story-modal__action" data-sheet-close>
        {{ trans('theme.common.close') }}
    </button>

    {% if story_product_id %}
    <button type="button"
            class="btn btn--neutral story-modal__action btn--wishlist not-added"
            data-id="{{ story_product_id }}"
            data-label-add="{{ trans('theme.product.add_to_wishlist') }}"
            data-label-remove="{{ trans('theme.product.remove_from_wishlist') }}"
            aria-pressed="false"
            onclick="salla.wishlist.toggle({{ story_product_id }})">
        <span data-wishlist-text>{{ trans('theme.product.add_to_wishlist') }}</span>
    </button>
    {% endif %}
{% endset %}

{% include 'components.ui.bottom-sheet' with {
    id:           id,
    title:        story_title,
    title_hidden: true,
    class:        'story-modal',
    body:         story_body,
    footer:       story_footer
} only %}
